          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When someone says they have heart failure, does that mean the heart has stopped working?",
          "answer": "No, not in the sense the words suggest. The heart has not stopped. According to the General clinical reference on heart failure, the name is misleading, and it \"does not mean the heart has stopped or is about to.\" It is a long-term condition that is usually managed, often for many years.\n\nThe same source explains what the term does mean. Heart failure is a state in which the heart cannot fill with or pump out blood well enough to meet the body's needs. Doctors sort it by ejection fraction, the share of blood the heart's main pumping chamber pushes out with each beat. In about half of cases the pumping is weak. In the other half the heart muscle is stiff and does not fill well. The source says this second type is \"not a milder form\" but a different problem.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not describe symptoms, causes, or how it is treated in detail. If you or someone you know has been told they have heart failure, a clinician can explain what it means for that particular person and what questions to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
